Optimizeed kernel config for Oracle Sunfire X5-2 server running KVM/Libvirt hypervisor.

This is forked from kernel.org stable.   I also add the forky (debian) patchset, including 
all of those that are relevant for this purpose.

The patchlist can be found in the forky_patchlist.txt file.  The forky patches and list can be 
pulled from https://salsa.debian.org/kernel-team/linux.git

So what did I do to the config?  I started with the default debian config kernel config-6.1.0-39-amd64 
(linux-image-6.1.0-39-amd64.deb)

#############################################################################

1. Kernel Configuration Optimizations

🧬 CPU / Scheduler

Enabled CONFIG_NO_HZ_FULL (full tickless) for isolated CPUs
Enabled CONFIG_RCU_NOCB_CPU for offloading RCU from isolated CPUs
Enabled CONFIG_SCHED_ISO (if present on your kernel base)
Enabled CONFIG_SCHED_AUTOGROUP=n to reduce scheduler jitter
Enabled CONFIG_HZ_1000 for high-resolution KVM host timing
Disabled unused scheduler debug configs

🧬 IOMMU / VT-d

Enabled Intel VT-d (CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU_DEFAULT_ON=y)
Enabled CONFIG_IRQ_REMAP=y
Enabled CONFIG_VFIO, CONFIG_VFIO_PCI, CONFIG_VFIO_NOIOMMU`

🧬 Block I/O / SCSI / Tape

Enabled SCSI passthrough optimizations
Removed all desktop storage drivers (USB mass storage, FireWire, etc.)
Kept megaraid_sas, mptsas, sg, st, and LTFS-critical paths
Disabled all MD/RAID, Bcache, and rarely-used block layers
Disabled rotational device heuristics
Defaulted I/O scheduler to none or mq-deadline for 10Gb NIC and tape backends

🧬 Networking

Enabled all Intel X540 (ixgbe) offloads
Enabled GRO, GSO, LRO hardware features
Disabled Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, consumer protocols
Disabled network debugging
Kept VXLAN, TAP, TUN, macvtap, bridging, vhost-net

🧬 General Decrufting

Removed sound subsystem
Removed ACPI video + GPU drivers
Removed DRM, fbcon, EFI fb
Removed all desktop filesystems (FUSE extras, exfat, msdos, udf, etc.)
Removed debugging frameworks
Removed tablet, HID-multimedia, bluetooth HID, and gamepad support
Removed unneeded USB drivers (camera, wifi, serial gadgets, etc.)
Removed wireless stacks completely
Leaned out kernel to reduce .text and slab footprint
Explicitly disabled virtualization guest drivers (only host drivers kept)

#############################################################################
2. Runtime System Tuning

A. IRQ and NUMA tuning

✔ IRQ balancer disabled for tuned devices

Wrote and deployed:
/usr/local/sbin/irq-optimizer  (see git project for it)

Moved interrupt-heavy NIC queues to match NUMA topology
Spread interrupts across correct local NUMA node
Isolated IRQs from isolated CPU pools

✔ CPU isolation (kernel command line)

  Added: isolcpus=2-15,34-47 nohz_full=2-15,34-47 rcu_nocbs=2-15,34-47 
  to the grub defaults.

B. Tape (LTFS) I/O tuning

Created sysctl entries to reduce tape latency and avoid I/O merging:

  /etc/sysctl.d/99-tape.conf
  vm.dirty_background_ratio = 2
  vm.dirty_ratio = 4
  vm.dirty_expire_centisecs = 500
  vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs = 100

These significantly reduce stall times for streaming tape workloads.

/usr/local/sbin/tape-tune.sh  (also will appear here when I commit them)

Configured to set:

  queue_depth=1
  nomerges=2
  scheduler=none

To help:

  * The tape block device (via scsi-generic)
  * All LTFS backends

#############################################################################

3. NIC tuning (ixgbe X540-AT2)

✔ Hardware offloads validated (ethtool)

  RX/TX checksum
  TSO/GSO/GRO
  Scatter-gather
  RSS with 63 queues
  Flow-director left off (correct for virtualization host)

✔ Ring sizes increased

I applied:

  ethtool -G eno3 rx 4096 tx 4096
  ethtool -G eno4 rx 4096 tx 4096

via a simple "bigrings" script added ti if-up.d

✔ Sysctl network tuning

/etc/sysctl.d/99-network.conf:

  net.core.rmem_max = 536870912
  net.core.wmem_max = 536870912
  net.core.netdev_max_backlog = 300000
  net.ipv4.tcp_rmem = 4096 87380 33554432
  net.ipv4.tcp_wmem = 4096 65536 33554432
  net.ipv4.tcp_low_latency = 1
  net.ipv4.tcp_mtu_probing = 1

#############################################################################

4. NUMA-aware VM Pinning Framework

Created:  /usr/local/sbin/pinvm

Capabilities:

  Auto-detect best NUMA node
  Score based on free mem + CPU load
  Apply host CPU pinning
  Apply guest vCPU pinning
  Pin emulator threads
  Show dry-run summary & prompt (y/n)

#############################################################################

5. System services and boot

* irqbalance disabled

     Because manual/NUMA IRQ placement is superior on dual-socket Xeon E5-2699 v3.
     Removed desktop boot services (fstrim.timer, cloud-init, etc.)

* Fixed UEFI /boot/efi mount issues on BIOS-mode VMs

#############################################################################




